<section xml:id="section-subgroups">
  <title>Subgroups</title>
  <p>
    I've already introduced two ways of capturing the structure of a
    group: Caley tables and conjugacy classes. Another way is to
    analyze its subgroups. First, the definition.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be a group. A subset <m>H \subset G</m> is a
        <term>subgroup</term> of <m>G</m> is <m>H</m> is itself a
        group under the same binary operation as <m>G</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        Equivalently, <m>H</m> is a subgroup if three things are true
        <ul>
          <li>
            <m>H</m> contains the group identity.
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>H</m> is closed under the binary operation. 
          </li>
          <li>
            For any <m>h \in H</m>, the inverse <m>h^{-1}</m> is also
            in <m>H</m>.
          </li>
        </ul>
        Additionally, if <m>H</m> is a proper subset of <m>G</m> (that
        is, <m>H \neq G</m>, then it is called a <term>propoer
        subgroup</term>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    In abstract algebra, the definition of a <sq>sub-object</sq> is
    frequently on of the first definitions. It's pretty natural to see
    what subsets of an object still fulfill the rules of that object.
    Note that the subgroup definition doesn't need to check
    associativity: the associativity of the operation is already known
    from the larger group <m>G</m>. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Let's get to some examples, using the various groups defined so
    far in the course. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Let <m>e \in G</m> be the identity element of a group. Then
        <m>\{e\}</m> is always a subgroup. As usual, mathematicians
        love our trivial cases: feel three to check that all the
        requirements of the definition are certainly met by the set
        that only contains the identity. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Many of the number sets were groups under addition. Since many
      of them are subsets of the larger number sets, these were all
      subgroups. <m>\ZZ</m> is a subgroup of <m>\QQ</m>, which is a
      subgroup of <m>\RR</m>, which is a subroup of <m>\CC</m>. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Something similar is true under multiplication. <m>\QQ^*</m> is
      a subgroup of <m>\RR^*</m>, which is a subgroup of <m>\CC^*</m>.
      The non-zero integers don't show up here, since they are not a
      group under multiplication. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      One might be tempted to say something like <m>\ZZ/n/\ZZ</m> is a
      subgroup of <m>\ZZ/m\ZZ</m> whenever <m>n \leq m</m>, or at least
      when <m>n</m> is a factor of <m>m</m>. Niether are true. The
      classes in <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> are integers modulo <m>n</m>, while the
      classes in <m>\ZZ/mZZ</m> are integers modulo <m>m</m>. If the
      two moduli are different, when these classes can't really be
      compared and are certainly not part of the same set at all. 
    </p>
    <p>
      That said, I can give some specific examples of subgroups of
      <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m>. For all of these, I leave it to you to verify
      that they are closed under addition, include the identity, and
      have all the inverses they need. 
      <ul>
        <li>
          <m>\{0,2,4,6\}</m> is a subgroup of <m>\ZZ/8\ZZ</m>. In
          general, the even classes are always a subgroup of
          <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> when <m>n</m> is even. 
        </li>
        <li>
          <m>\{0,3,6,9\}</m> is a subgroup of <m>\ZZ/12</m>. 
        </li>
        <li>
          <m>\{0,5,10,15,20\}</m> is a subgroup  of <m>\ZZ/25</m>.
        </li>
        <li>
          You may be already thinking about various propositions about
          subgroups of <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> based on these examples: there
          certainly are patterns here. After doing some more
          definition, I'll state and prove some propositions about
          subgroups of cyclic groups, which will generalize some of
          the patterns you might see here.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>\{1,9\}</m>, <m>\{1,11\}</m>, <m>\{1,19</m>, and
      <m>\{1,3,7,9\}</m> are subgroups of <m>\ZZ/20\ZZ^*</m>. You can
      verify the subgroup properties using the Caley table <xref
      ref="example-caley-z20-units"/>. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The set of real-coefficient polynomial of degree at most 2,
      is a subgroup of <m>\RR[x]</m>. The identity is the zero
      polynomial, which has degree zero, so it is included. 
      I can never get a higher degree polynomial by adding quadratics,
      and the inverse (under addition) of a quadratic is another
      quadratic. Note that <sq>at most</sq> is important here. I can
      add two quadratics and get a linear polynomial, as in this
      example: <m>x^2 + (-x^2 + x) = x</m>, so I need linear and
      constant polynomials to make sure this subset is closed under
      addition.
    </p>
    <p>
      There was nothing special about 2 in the previous paragraph. The
      set of real-coefficient polynomials od degree at most <m>n</m>,
      for any <m>n \in \NN</m>, is a subgroup of <m>\RR[x]</m>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This examples work with other coefficients as well: in
      <m>\ZZ[x]</m>, in <m>\QQ[x]</m> and so on. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      There are many subgroups in the function groups, both under
      pointwise operations and under composition. In general, if the
      domain and codomains are fixed, then moving to more restrictive
      properties gives subgroups. For example:
      <me>  
        C^{\infty}(\RR) \subset \ldots \subset C^2(\RR) \subset
        C^1(\RR) \subset C(\RR) 
      </me>
      The continuous functions (under pointwise addition) are the most
      general group in this list. Moving to continuously
      differentiable functions is a restriction, so a subset. But the
      inverse of a continuously differentiable function is still
      continuously differentiable, and likewise for sums of such
      functions. So this is a subgroup. Then being twice continuously
      differentiable is a stronger conditions yet, then three times,
      four times, and so on to infinitely differentiable functions. At
      each step, the zero function is still present and the inverses
      and sums stay in the same subset, so this is subgroups all the
      way down. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This can be repeated for any other real domain as well, of
      course. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Under composition, something similar happens. 
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \{f: \RR \rightarrow \RR | f \text{ is bijective and
          infinitely differentiable} \} \subset 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \vdots
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{f: \RR \rightarrow \RR | f \text{ is bijective and
           twice continuously differentiable} \} \subset 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{f: \RR \rightarrow \RR | f \text{ is bijective and
           continuusly  differentiable} \} \subset 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{f: \RR \rightarrow \RR | f \text{ is bijective and
           continuusly} \} \subset 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{f: \RR \rightarrow \RR | f \text{ is bijective} \}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      This is again a cascade of subgroups. Since I am working with
      compositions, I need to assume that these are bijective at each
      step: functions under composition are invertible if and only if
      they are bijective. After that assumption, though, I can impose
      strong and stronger assumption to make subgroups. At each steps,
      the subgroups are closed under composition, include the identity
      function (which is the identity under composition), and have
      inverses of the same properties. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      These are all the subgroups of <m>D_4</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id\}, \{\Id, F_1\}, \{\Id, F_2\}, \{\Id, F_3\}, \{\Id,
          F_4\}, \{\Id, R_2\}, \{\Id, R_1, R_2, R_3\}, 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, R_2, F_1, F_3\}, \{\Id, R_2, F_2, F_4\}, D_4
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <me>
      </me>
      This can be verified, again, from the Caley table <xref
      ref="table-d4-multiplication"/>. Doing so is a fair bit of work
      (to do this the most naive way would be to test all <m>2^8 =
      256</m> possible subsets to see which satisfied the subgroup
      properties), but for finite groups, it is nice to at least have
      the possibility of exhaustively checking. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      These are all the subgroups of <m>D_5</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id\}, \{\Id, F_1\}, \{\Id, F_2\}, \{\Id, F_3\}, \{\Id,
          F_4\}, \{\Id, F_5\}, 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, R_1, R_2, R_3, R_4\}, D_5
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <me>
      </me>
      Note that even though there are more elements in <m>D_5</m> than
      <m>D_4</m>, there actually fewer subgroups. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The matrix groups (classical groups) also nicely fit subgroup
      relations. Here are a few of those chains of subgroups. 
      <md>
        <mrow>
          SO(n) \subset SL(n) \subset GL(n)
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          SO(n) \subset O(n) \subset GL(n) 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          GL(\ZZ,n) \subset GL(\QQ,n) \subset GL(\RR,n) \subset
          GL(\CC,n) 
        </mrow>
      </md>
      All of these subsets are infact subgroups. The identity is
      matrix is contained in each. Each is closed under matrix
      multiplication, and the inverses remain in the subsets for each
      of these.
    </p>
    <p>
      The different between the first and second chains of subgroups
      is interesting. <m>SO(n)</m> is a subgroup of both <m>SL(n)</m>
      and <m>O(n)</m>. But there is no subgroup relationship between
      <m>SL(n)</m> and <m>O(0n)</m>. Neither is a subset of the
      either, even though they both lie in <m>GL(n)</m>. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>A_n</m> is a subgroup of <m>S_n</m>. The identity is an even
      permutation, the product of two even permutations remains even,
      and the inverse of an even permutation is even: <m>A_n</m>
      satisfies the subgroup rules. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Having hopefully given a rich basis of examples, let me move on to
    some definitions that help to understand subgroup structures.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be a group and let <m>A \subset G</m> be a
        subset. The <term>subgroup generated by <m>A</m></term> is the
        smallest subgroup of <m>G</m> that contain <m>A</m>. The
        notation for this <m>\langle \rangle</m>. If <m>A</m> is a
        singleton or a small finite set, then the notation is usually
        <m>\langle a \rangle</m> or <m>\langle a,b,c \rangle</m>
        instead of the more technically correct <m>\langle \{a\}
        \rangle</m> or <m>\langle \{a,b,c\} \rangle</m>. A subgroup
        generated by a single element is a <term>cyclic
        subgroup</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    There are some subtleties with this definition, and with the word
    <sq>generated</sq>. I'm going to address these subtleties with a
    proposition, which I won't prove here for sake of time. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be a group and let <m>A \subset G</m> be a
        subset. Then consider the following three constructions.
        <ul>
          <li>
            The subgroup generated by <m>A</m> as I just defined it:
            the smallest subgroup that contains the set <m>A</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            The set of finite products (using whatever the group
            binary operation is, of course) of elements of <m>A</m> and
            their inverses. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The intersection of all subgroups of <m>G</m> which
            contain <m>A</m>. 
          </li>
        </ul>
        These three constructions are identical, and the term
        <sq>subgroup generated by <m>A</m></sq> can be understood in
        any of these three ways. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    The equivalence of the first and the second is important here. The
    first is a nice clear definition: the smallest subgroups that
    contains a subset. But it doesn't actually capture the sense of
    <sq>generated</sq> that informed <xref
    ref="definition-generated"/>. In that definition, generated means
    that you can get everything in the group (or now subgroup) as
    product of the generators and their inverses. The proposition
    tells me that I can still using <sq>generated</sq> in this
    constructive sense, which is very good. 
  </p>
  <p>
    This is also a good example of constructive and non-constructive
    definitions. The definition of a subgroup generated by a subset
    that I gave is nonconstructive. It makes sense that a minimal
    subgroup containing a set exists, but the definition doesn't give
    you any idea of what that subgroup it nor how to calculate it. But
    the alternative definition, where the subgroups is explicitly the
    things you can get as products of elements of the subset and their
    inverses, actually shows you how to build the subgroup. It
    demonstrates a construction.  
  </p>
  <p>
    This definition is very useful: many of the subgroups we talk
    about in this course will be presented as subgroups generated by
    elements. But we must be careful. Let me return to the dihedral
    example to demonstrate. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      These are all the subgroups of <m>D_4</m>, but now written with
      generatores. 
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id\} \amp = \langle \emptyset \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, F_1\} \amp = \langle F_1 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, F_2\} \amp = \langle F_2 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, F_3\} \amp = \langle F_3 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, F_4\} \amp = \langle F_4 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, R_2\} \amp = \langle R_2 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, R_1, R_2, R_3\}
          \amp = \langle R_1 \rangle
          = \langle R_3 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, R_2, F_1, F_3\}, \amp = \langle R_2, F_1 \rangle
          = \langle R_2, F_3 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
         \{\Id, R_2, F_2, F_4\}, \amp = \langle  R_2, F_2
         \rangle = \langle  R_2, F_4 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
         D_4 \amp = 
         \langle  R_1, F_4 \rangle = 
         \langle  R_3, F_2 \rangle = \text{many others}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      There are a number of useful things to point out from this
      example. First, the same group can be described by different
      sets of generates, as the last three examples show. Second, there
      are several cyclic subgroups of various orders, but not all
      subgroups are cyclic: the last two subgroups require two
      generator. Third and most importantly, it can be tricky to see,
      at a glance, what kind of subgroup is generated by certain
      elements. <m>\langle R_1 \rangle</m> is a subgroup of order 4, but
      <m>\langle R_2 \rangle</m> is a subgroup of order 2. <m>\langle
      R_2,F_4 \rangle</m> is a subgroup of order 4, but <m>\langle
      R_1,F_4 \rangle</m> is actually all of <m>D_4</m>. It's good to
      be careful with subgroups description by generations <mdash/> to
      be hesitant to jump to conclusions about the order of subgroups
      generated by elements. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>quaternion group</term> is the set
        <m>Q = \{1,-1,i,-i,j,-j,k,-k\}</m> with the following Caley
        table.  
        <me>
          \begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
            \cdot \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp i \amp -i \amp j \amp -j \amp k
            \amp -k \\
            \hline
            1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp i \amp -i \amp j \amp -j \amp k \amp
            -k \\[0.2em]
            -1 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp -i \amp i \amp -j \amp j \amp -k \amp 
            k\\[0.2em]
            i \amp i \amp -i \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp k \amp -k \amp -j \amp 
            j\\[0.2em]
            -i \amp -i \amp i \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp -k \amp k \amp j \amp 
            -j \\[0.2em]
            j \amp j \amp -j \amp -k \amp k \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp i \amp 
            -i\\[0.2em]
            -j \amp -j \amp j \amp k \amp -k \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp -i \amp 
            i\\[0.2em]
            k \amp k \amp -k \amp j \amp -j \amp -i \amp i \amp -1 \amp 
            1\\[0.2em]
            -k \amp -k \amp k \amp -j \amp j \amp i \amp -i \amp 1 \amp 
            -1
          \end{array}
        </me>
        This table is a bit strange to parse. It's easy to remember
        that all of <m>i</m>, <m>j</m> and <m>k</m> square to
        <m>-1</m>, and that multiplying them together in order gives
        the next in alphabet order (looping around from <m>k</m> to
        <m>i</m>). 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            i^2 = j^2 = k^2 = -1 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            ij = k \hspace{1cm} jk = i \hspace{1cm} ki = j 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            ji = -k \hspace{1cm} kj = -i \hspace{1cm} ik = -j 
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <example>
    <p>
      These are the subgroups of <m>Q</m>.
    </p>
  </example>
</section>
